PART 1 — THE BUG (no lock, two students take the last place)
======================================================================
  offering_id=521  capacity=1  student_A=1030  student_B=1031
  T1 outcome: ('COMMITTED', None)
  T2 outcome: ('COMMITTED', None)
  Active rows after = 2  (capacity = 1)
  >>> BUG REPRODUCED: capacity=1 but two ACTIVE rows committed.
======================================================================
PART 2 — CONSTRAINT FIX (counter row + CHECK(active_count <= capacity))
======================================================================
  offering_id=522  capacity=1  student_A=1030  student_B=1031
  T1 outcome: ('FAILED', '23514', 'new row for relation "race_demo_capacity" violates check constraint "race_demo_capacity_check"\nDETAIL:  Failing row cont')
  T2 outcome: ('COMMITTED', None)
  Active rows after = 1  (capacity = 1)
  >>> FIX CONFIRMED: only one ACTIVE row landed.
======================================================================
PART 3 — ADVISORY LOCK FIX (pg_advisory_xact_lock(offering_id))
======================================================================
  offering_id=523  capacity=1  student_A=1030  student_B=1031
  T1 outcome: ('FAILED', '23514', 'offering 523 is full (capacity 1, active 1)\nCONTEXT:  PL/pgSQL function enforce_capacity() line 17 at RAISE')
  T2 outcome: ('COMMITTED', None)
  Active rows after = 1  (capacity = 1)
  >>> FIX CONFIRMED: only one ACTIVE row landed; the other was blocked.
======================================================================
PART 4 — SERIALIZABLE isolation (expects SQLSTATE 40001)
======================================================================
  offering_id=524  capacity=1  student_A=1030  student_B=1031
  T1 outcome: ('COMMITTED', None)
  T2 outcome: ('ABORTED', '40001', 'could not serialize access due to read/write dependencies among transactions\nDETAIL:  Reason code: Canceled on identific')
  >>> PASS: at least one transaction aborted with SQLSTATE 40001.
======================================================================
PART 5 — READ COMMITTED vs REPEATABLE READ (snapshot behaviour)
======================================================================
  5a. READ COMMITTED — a second read inside the same transaction
      sees another transaction's committed write.
      READ COMMITTED reads: [('first_read', 0), ('second_read', 1)]
      >>> PASS: READ COMMITTED saw the writer's committed value.
  5b. REPEATABLE READ — a second read inside the same transaction
      uses the original snapshot (writer's update is invisible).
      REPEATABLE READ reads: [('first_read', 0), ('second_read', 0)]
      >>> PASS: REPEATABLE READ kept the snapshot value.
======================================================================
PART 6 — DEADLOCK (40P01) and the fixed-order version
======================================================================
  6a. Buggy version — opposite update orders.
      T1 outcome: ('COMMITTED', None)
      T2 outcome: ('ABORTED', '40P01', 'deadlock detected\nDETAIL:  Process 18716 waits for ShareLock on transaction 857; blocked by process 18715.\nProcess 18715')
      >>> PASS: PostgreSQL detected the deadlock (SQLSTATE 40P01).
  6b. Fixed version — same update order on both threads.
      T1 outcome: ('COMMITTED', None)
      T2 outcome: ('COMMITTED', None)
      >>> PASS: both transactions committed without a deadlock.
